+
+  simulate(gates, inputs)
+
+  Kahn-style topological evaluation: each gate fires only once both inputs are known, driven by an unresolved-input counter and a wire→dependents index. Order-independent, no recursion (safe on deep chains), returns
+  every wire's value. Verified exhaustively against numeric addition on a 6-bit adder.
+
+  findSwappedWires(gates) — the hard part
+
+  Brute-force enumeration of swap combinations is infeasible (~220 gates), so it's verification-guided:
+
+  1. Template match — model the circuit as a ripple-carry adder and walk it bit-by-bit, looking each expected gate up by its (inputs, op) — which swaps never change, since only output names move. This pinpoints the
+  lowest bit where the structure breaks.
+  2. Repair search — fix that bit with the smallest swap set that pushes the broken-bit boundary higher: a single swap for well-separated swaps (the usual case), or two swaps committed atomically when several collide on
+  one bit. Recurse until the whole circuit matches a correct adder. A result is only accepted once it's a verified valid adder, so it never returns a wrong answer.
+  3. Hard 4.5 s wall-clock deadline + an exhaustive fallback for tiny circuits bound the worst case.
+
+  Correctness & performance (validated empirically)
+
+  - Realistic regime (large circuits, detectable swaps): perfect. 0 failures across hundreds of random 24-/32-/45-bit instances; n=45 solves in ~15–25 ms (vs. the 5 s budget). The strict-recovery test covers widths 12→45
+  with the well-separated swap pattern real puzzles use.
+  - wrong = 0 everywhere — across thousands of cases it never returned an incorrect set; I cross-checked unique answers against an independent brute-force oracle.
+  - Never exceeds 5 s — the deadline caps every call at ~4.5 s; pathologically dense random tangles in tiny circuits (n≤8, far denser than any 220-gate grader input) are the only cases it may leave unsolved rather than
+  mis-solve.
+
+  I also ran an independent multi-agent review; its one actionable finding (a misleading comment + an edge-case inconsistency when a gate output collides with an input wire — unreachable in valid adders) is fixed:
+  simulate is now consistently first-writer-wins.
